<h3>Dear <%= @user.full_name %></h3>

<p>
    Please click on the link below to login. This link will expire in
    <%= UserMailer::MAGIC_LINK_TTL.in_minutes.to_i %> minutes.
</p>

<%
    @current_entity ||= nil

    # Security fix (Finding 5 — silent email failure / NoMethodError):
    # The original code called `puts "...: #{@current_entity.id}"` which:
    #   1. Raised NoMethodError on nil in Ruby 3.x when current_entity_id was absent (main
    #      domain logins). The background job crashed silently — the user was told "Login link
    #      sent" but received nothing, effectively denying magic-link authentication.
    #   2. Logged entity ID and subdomain to stdout on every magic link request in production.
    # Fixed by using safe navigation (.present?) and removing the debug puts entirely.
    subdomain = @current_entity.present? && !Rails.env.test? ? @current_entity.sub_domain : nil

    # Security fix (Finding 6 — expiry mismatch): token TTL now driven by the same constant
    # as the subject line (UserMailer::MAGIC_LINK_TTL = 10 minutes). Previously the subject
    # said "Expires in 5 minutes" but signed_id used 15.minutes, leaving an undisclosed
    # extra window during which an intercepted link could be replayed.
    # https://kukicola.io/posts/signed-urls-with-ruby/
    signed_id = @user.signed_id(expires_in: UserMailer::MAGIC_LINK_TTL)
%>

<p class="btn btn-outline-primary">
    <%#
      Security fix (Finding 7 — redirect_to phishing): redirect_to has been removed from
      this URL. Previously, whatever value was passed as params[:redirect_to] to the
      POST /users/magic_link endpoint was embedded here verbatim. An unauthenticated attacker
      could supply any internal path; the victim — receiving an email from our legitimate mail
      server — would be redirected there after authenticating without any visible warning.

      The destination after login is now determined exclusively from session[:user_return_to]
      server-side in UsersController#no_password_login. That value is set by Devise when it
      intercepts an unauthenticated request, lives only in the server session store, and is
      never attacker-controlled through the magic link flow.
    %>
    <%= link_to 'Click To Login', no_password_login_users_url(signed_id:, subdomain:) %>
</p>

<p>
    Please do NOT share this email with others.
</p>
